## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory layout, overlap handling, and cache-friendly copy strategies in tight, real-world paths.

## Key Concepts
- Overlap-safe memmove
- Cache-friendly 64-byte blocks
- Alignment assumptions and tail handling
- Fallback to memcpy for tiny or unaligned data
- Microbenchmark plan to measure throughput vs standard memcpy

## Code Example
```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *memmove64(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n){
  unsigned char *d = (unsigned char*)dst;
  const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char*)src;
  const size_t BLOCK = 64;

  if ((size_t)(d) <= (size_t)(s) || d >= s + n){
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      d += BLOCK; s += BLOCK; n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d, s, n);
  } else {
    d += n; s += n;
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      d -= BLOCK; s -= BLOCK;
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d - n, s - n, n);
  }
  return dst;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for arbitrary alignment and larger data sizes?
- Compare performance to builtin memmove on x86-64 with different runtimes.
- What edge cases or architectures could break this approach?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of low-level synchronization, memory ordering, and backoff strategies under contention. Also checks ability to translate theory into working code and simple performance measurement.

## Key Concepts
- C11 atomics and memory_order_acquire/release
- atomic_flag spinlocks and their correctness
- exponential backoff to prevent livelock
- basic contention metrics (wall time, backoffs)

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <emmintrin.h>

static atomic_uint counter = 0;
static atomic_flag lock = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT;

void lock_spin() {
  unsigned int backoff = 1;
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&lock, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    if (backoff < 1024) backoff <<= 1;
    for (volatile unsigned int i = 0; i < backoff; ++i) _mm_pause();
  }
}

void unlock_spin() {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&lock, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
The barrier tests fundamental atomic sequencing and memory-order choices without OS primitives.

## Key Concepts
- Barrier reuse with generation counter
- Atomic increments and acquire/release semantics
- False sharing avoidance via padding
- Stress testing for thread skew and latency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo:
volatile int arrivals; // atomic
volatile int generation; // atomic

void barrier_enter(int N) {
  int a = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&arrivals, 1, memory_order_acq_rel);
  if (a + 1 == N) {
    atomic_store_explicit(&arrivals, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&generation, 1, memory_order_release);
  }
  int g = atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire);
  while (g == atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire)) { ; }
}
```

